------------------------------------------------------------------------

This appendix reports the full model output supporting the meso-level analysis of Economic immigration attitudes. All models use individual and wave fixed effects with standard errors clustered at the respondent level. The outcome throughout is `immigEcon`, which asks respondents whether immigration undermines or enriches British Economic life; higher values indicate more permissive attitudes. The structure mirrors Appendix B exactly: Section B.9.1 presents the main Britishness models for all three constituency-level economic indicators; Section B.9.2 reports the categorical Britishness robustness check; Section B.9.3 presents the parallel Englishness models; and Section B.9.4 reports the categorical Englishness robustness check. A description of the meso-level variables appears in Section B.9.5.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B.9.1 Main Models: Britishness (Continuous) {#sec-b8-brit}

## B.9.1.1 Local Unemployment Rate {#sec-b8-brit-unemp}

Across the baseline specifications, the local unemployment rate exhibits no statistically significant main effect on economic immigration attitudes once individual and wave fixed effects are included. Once the interaction with Britishness is introduced, a conditional pattern emerges: the unemployment main effect is positive and significant (β ≈ 0.015, p \< 0.05), indicating that higher local unemployment is associated with more permissive economic immigration attitudes at the highest levels of Britishness, while the negative and significant interaction term (β ≈ −0.003, p \< 0.05) indicates that this association attenuates as Britishness decreases. The Britishness main effect is non-significant in the interaction models. This pattern is robust to the inclusion of age controls.

```{r b8-brit-unemp-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Unemp only"    = m_u0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness" = m_u1,
    "(3) Unemp + Age"   = m_u0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness" = m_u2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", unemployment_rate = "Unemp. rate (local)", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.1. Local Unemployment Rate and Economic Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigEcon.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b8-brit-unemp-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_u3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_u4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", unemployment_rate = "Unemp. rate (local)",
                  "brit:unemployment_rate" = "Britishness × Unemp. rate", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.2. Local Unemployment Rate × Britishness: Interaction Models (Economic DV)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigEcon.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

## B.9.1.2 Claimant Count {#sec-b8-brit-claimant}

Main-effect specifications show a small but significant positive unconditional association between claimant intensity and economic immigration attitudes (β ≈ 0.010, p \< 0.05), which persists with and without age controls. In the interaction models, the claimant main effect remains positive and significant (β ≈ 0.013, p \< 0.05) and the Britishness main effect is negative and significant (β ≈ −0.011, p \< 0.01), but the interaction term is negative and non-significant (β ≈ −0.001, p \> 0.1) in both specifications. There is therefore no evidence of conditional moderation by Britishness for the claimant indicator with the economic immigration DV.

```{r b8-brit-claimant-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Claimant only"  = m_c0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness"  = m_c1,
    "(3) Claimant + Age" = m_c0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness"  = m_c2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", claimant_rate = "Claimant count (local)", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.3. Claimant Count and Economic Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigEcon.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b8-brit-claimant-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_c3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_c4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", claimant_rate = "Claimant count (local)",
                  "brit:claimant_rate" = "Britishness × Claimant count", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.4. Claimant Count × Britishness: Interaction Models (Economic DV)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigEcon.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

## B.9.1.3 Local Income (ASHE) {#sec-b8-brit-income}

In contrast to the unemployment and claimant results, local income exhibits a significant and consistent interaction with Britishness. Main-effect specifications show no significant unconditional income association. In the interaction models, however, both the income main effect (β ≈ 0.001, p \< 0.001) and the Britishness main effect (β ≈ 0.069, p \< 0.001) are positive and highly significant, while the Britishness × local income interaction is negative and highly significant (β ≈ −0.000, p \< 0.001), robust to age controls. This indicates that higher local earnings are associated with more permissive economic immigration attitudes among respondents with weaker national identification, with the association attenuating as Britishness increases. This pattern closely parallels the income interaction observed for immigSelf in Appendix B.1.

```{r b8-brit-income-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Income only"   = m_i0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness" = m_i1,
    "(3) Income + Age"  = m_i0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness" = m_i2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", earnings_median = "Local income (ASHE)", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.5. Local Income (ASHE) and Economic Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigEcon.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b8-brit-income-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_i3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_i4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", earnings_median = "Local income (ASHE)",
                  "brit:earnings_median" = "Britishness × Local income", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.6. Local Income (ASHE) × Britishness: Interaction Models (Economic DV)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigEcon.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B.9.2 Robustness: Britishness (Categorical) {#sec-b8-brit-cat}

Re-estimating the economic DV meso models using Britishness as a categorical variable (reference: 7) partially confirms the conclusions from the continuous specifications, though with some important qualifications.

For local unemployment, the categorical interaction models reveal significant positive terms concentrated at intermediate to stronger identity positions — brit=4 (β ≈ 0.013, p \< 0.01), brit=5 (β ≈ 0.015, p \< 0.001), and brit=6 (β ≈ 0.017, p \< 0.001) — consistent with the continuous interaction result and indicating that the positive within-person unemployment association attenuates as Britishness decreases from the reference category. Interaction terms at weaker identity positions are non-significant.

For claimant count, categorical interaction models yield no systematic pattern. Only brit=4 reaches significance (β ≈ 0.011, p \< 0.01), with all other terms non-significant, consistent with the null continuous interaction result in B.9.1.2.

For local income, the categorical interaction models reveal a structured and highly significant gradient. Interaction terms earnings_median × brit=1 through brit=5 are all positive and significant, declining in magnitude toward the reference category, while brit=6 is non-significant. This confirms that the income moderation effect observed in the continuous specification reflects a systematic gradient across identity positions, with the attenuation of the negative income main effect most pronounced among the weakest Britishness respondents.

