# B.9.4 Robustness: Englishness (Categorical) {#sec-b8-engl-cat}

Replacing continuous Englishness with a categorical specification (reference: 7) broadly confirms the gradient structure documented in Sections B.9.1 and B.9.3. Englishness category coefficients are positive, precisely estimated, and monotonically ordered across all three indicators in the main-effects models.

For local unemployment, categorical interaction models show significant positive terms across all identity positions from engl=1 through engl=6, with no monotonic ordering — the largest effects appear at engl=2 and engl=4 (β ≈ 0.029–0.032, p \< 0.001) rather than strictly at the weakest positions. This indicates that the attenuation of the negative unemployment main effect at the reference category is present across the full identity distribution, consistent with the continuous interaction result.

For claimant count, the categorical interaction pattern is not systematic. Significant positive terms appear at engl=1 (β ≈ 0.012, p \< 0.05) and engl=4 (β ≈ 0.010, p \< 0.05), with all other terms non-significant, consistent with the null continuous interaction in B.9.3.2 and indicating no coherent identity gradient.

For local income, categorical interaction models reveal a structured pattern. Interaction terms earnings_median × engl=1 through engl=5 are all positive and significant, declining toward the reference category, while engl=6 is non-significant — closely mirroring the Britishness categorical income result in B.9.2.3 and confirming that the income moderation effect reflects a systematic gradient across Englishness positions.

